      "urpx:forRatePlan": {
        "@id": "gpc:tou-sc"
      },
      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "gpc:cc-tou-sc-2026-06-01",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "gpc:cc-tou-sc-2026-06-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 7 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Voltage level and franchise fee location. FCR-27's three voltage levels and MFF-11's two locations are crossed in one ProfileAlternatives set of six alternatives, because URPX v0.5.1 allows one set per PlanElements and the franchise fee's energy subtotal must include that voltage level's fuel prices. A consumer selects one alternative, for example Secondary Distribution inside city limits. (2) Municipal Franchise Fee base. MFF-11 applies to the total revenues of each bill, which include the ECCR-15 and DSM-C-15 amounts. Its multiplier Prices here are based on the energy-and-fuel subtotal and on the non-energy charges' Prices, not on the ECCR and DSM-C multiplier Prices, so the franchise fee on those two amounts, about 0.44 percent of the base bill inside city limits, is not carried. (3) Excess Reactive Demand. Where a power factor below 95 percent lagging is indicated, the Company may install metering for reactive demand and bills kVAR above one-third of the measured kW at $0.43 per kVAR. The JSON-LD carries the $0.43 on a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod; a consumer has to compute the excess kVAR, and supplies none for a customer the Company does not meter for it. (4) Customer-specific Off-Peak rate. Under the Standard Price Option the Off-Peak rate is computed by the Company from the cost to serve the customer's load and location, to meet the Company's financial requirements, and is reviewed after three years or the Initial Period if usage moves more than 15 percent from the initial projections. The JSON-LD defines Off-Peak energy as a PriceDefinition with no Price, so a modeled bill carries no Off-Peak energy charge, and the ECCR, DSM and franchise fee on it are missing too. (5) Flat Price Option and Monthly Access Charge Option. At the Company's option a customer may instead pay a customer-specific flat energy rate for an Initial Period, inclusive of the Basic Service Charge, the riders and a risk adder of up to 10 percent; or, for large loads, a customer-specific Monthly Access Charge plus a $155 per month Administrative Charge, with all energy at the hourly Real Time Pricing - Day Ahead price. Neither is carried. (6) Minimum Monthly Bill. Under the Standard Price Option the sheet sets it at the $174.88 Basic Service Charge plus ECCR, DSM and the Municipal Franchise Fee, and no Price carries it. Every price here is positive, so a bill on these prices alone is never below it. (7) Term of Contract. Three years, or as specified in the customer's Contract for Electric Service. Nothing is priced by it and it is not carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-19T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
